      <eNarrative>
        <eNarrative.01>Dispatched for stroke-like symptoms at a private residence, arriving to find a 68-year-old male sitting in a chair with his spouse at bedside. Spouse reports patient was last known well at 0650 and was witnessed to develop sudden right facial droop, right arm weakness, and difficulty speaking at approximately 0715, well within the thrombolytic treatment window. On arrival at 0736 patient was alert, following commands, but with obvious expressive aphasia limiting his ability to communicate history or needs verbally. Cincinnati Prehospital Stroke Scale was positive for all three components: right facial droop, right arm drift, and abnormal (aphasic) speech. Given the acute focal neurologic deficit, unstable and rapidly time-sensitive presentation, and inability to reliably protect his airway or safely self-transport given the motor and speech deficits, transport by private vehicle was contraindicated; ALS ambulance transport with continuous cardiac and neurologic monitoring was medically necessary to enable rapid, monitored transport to a comprehensive stroke center capable of advanced stroke intervention. Initial vitals showed hypertension (BP 178/-) with SpO2 97% and heart rate 82, prompting continuous SpO2 and cardiac monitoring during transport to detect any deterioration or arrhythmia. Blood glucose was checked at 104 mg/dL to rule out hypoglycemia as a stroke mimic, allowing the crew to proceed confidently with the stroke pathway. Two peripheral IV lines were established en route in anticipation of thrombolytic or interventional therapy on arrival, avoiding delay at the receiving facility. A stroke alert was transmitted from the scene to the receiving comprehensive stroke center with time last known well, exam findings, and estimated arrival time so the stroke team could mobilize prior to patient arrival, which is only feasible with direct EMS notification during transport. Repeat vitals at 24 minutes showed improved blood pressure trend (170/-), stable SpO2 98%, and heart rate 78, with the patient remaining alert and hemodynamically stable throughout transport, though the expressive aphasia and right-sided weakness persisted unchanged. The patient was transported emergently, without incident, and care was transferred to the stroke team with full report at the comprehensive stroke center for time-critical evaluation and treatment.</eNarrative.01>
      </eNarrative>
